{"id": "q0009", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 14, "onset_chunk": 13, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Shelly has 27 plus 12 painting s, which is 39. She lly has eight paintings more than Kim, so Kim has 3 9 minus eight painting s, which is thirty-one paintings. The answer is 31.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 13, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0017", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 22, "onset_chunk": 22, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Madeline ate 6 grape s. Her brother used five times as many, so that' s 5 times 6 equal s 3 0 grapes for the juice. Together, they used 6 plus 30, which is 36 gra pes. Their mother made four pie s, each needing 1 2 grapes, so that's 4 times 12 equal s 48 gra pes for the pies. Adding the grapes used by Madeline, her brother, and their mother gives us 36 plus 4 8, which equal s 84 gra pes in total. So there were 84 grape s at the beginning.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 30, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0031", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 15, "onset_chunk": 15, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So we have 2 bags of chips with 5 5 chips each. That means there are a total of 2 times 5 5, which is 110 chips in all. If the family has 5 members and they want to split them equally, then each person gets 11 0 divided by 5, which equals 2 2 chips. Each person gets 2 2 chips.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 20, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0035", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 28, "onset_chunk": 23, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so let's figure this out. First, we need to see how long it takes for the old varnish to dry on six painting s. Since each painting takes 7 minutes with the old varnish, and assuming she applies them one after another, the total time would be 6 times 7, which is 42 minutes.Now, for the new varnish, each painting takes 1 2 minutes. So for six paintings, that ’s 6 times 12, equal ing 72 minutes. To find the difference, we subtract the old time from the new time: 7 2 minus 42 equal s 30 minutes.So, it will take 30 minutes longer for the new varnish to dry on six paintings compared to the old one.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 37, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0044", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 34, "onset_chunk": 33, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So on the first day, there were 50 people. On the second day, 2 0 more than the first, so that's 7 0 people. On the third day, it was twice the number of the second day, so that' s 1 40 people. Now, for the rest of the week, which is four days left after those three, he saw an average of 6 0 people each day. So that would be 6 0 times 4, which is 240 people. Adding all of them up: 50 plus 70 plus 1 40 is 26 0, and then plus 2 40 gives us a total of 500 people. The total number of people Connor saw at the beach that week was 5 00.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 38, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0051", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 18, "onset_chunk": 17, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So Gary bought a boat for 9 000 dollars. Over the first year it depreciated 30%. So we can calculate that as 9 000 times 1 minus 0. 3, which is equal to 6 30 0 dollars. The second year it de preciated another 3 0%, so we can calculate that as 6 3 00 times 1 minus 0.3, which is equal to 4 410 dollars. The third year it depreciated 20%, so we can calculate that as 4 410 times 1 minus 0.2, which is equal to 3 5 28 dollars. Therefore, the boat is worth 3 5 28 dollars after three years.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 44, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0067", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 14, "onset_chunk": 13, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "He has a quarter, which is 25 cents. Two nick els are worth 10 cents. Seven dimes are worth 7 0 cents. Add ing them together: 2 5 + 1 0 + 70 equals 10 5 cents.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 13, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0072", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 13, "onset_chunk": 12, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, let's break this down step by step. George has 18 pen s. Timothy has three times the number of pen s that George owns. So, Timothy has 3 times 18, which is 54 pens. Sarah has half as many pens as Timot hy. Half of 5 4 is 27. So, Sarah has 2 7 pens.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 20, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0076", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 22, "onset_chunk": 21, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So, Ryan's allowance is six dollars each week he does his cho res. He did his chores for three weeks, so that's six times three, which is 1 8 dollars total. Then he bought ice cream cones for himself and three friends, so that' s four people in total. Each cone costs one dollar twenty five, so four times one dollar twenty five is five dollars spent on ice cream. That leaves him with 1 8 minus five, which is thirteen dollars left. Now, movie tickets cost six dollars and fifty cents each. If we divide the remaining money by the ticket price, thirteen divided by six point five gives us two tickets. So, Ryan can buy two movie tickets.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 37, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0090", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 19, "onset_chunk": 18, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So Kim raise s 3 2 0 dollars more than Alex andra, who raised 4 30 dollars. So Kim raised 4 3 0 plus 3 20 dollars, which is 7 5 0 dollars. Mariam raises 40 0 dollars more than Sarah, who raised 300 dollars, so Mari am raised 300 plus 400 dollars, which is 700 dollars. So in total they all raised 43 0 for Alexan dra, 75 0 for Kim, and 70 0 for Mariam, which add s up to 1 9 80 dollars.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 33, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0098", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 14, "onset_chunk": 13, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Let's break this down. We know there are 20 animals total, and they're all either chickens or cows. Chick ens have two legs each, and cows have four legs each. If we let C equal the number of chicken s, then the number of cow s would be 2 0 minus C, because together they add up to 20. Now, for the legs: 2 times the number of chickens plus 4 times the number of cows equals 70. Sub stituting in, we get 2C plus 4 times 20 minus C equal s 7 0. That simpl ifies to 2C plus 80 minus 4 C equals 70. Com b ine like terms: -2 C plus 8 0 equals 7 0. Sub tract 80 from both sides, getting -2 C equal s -10. Divide by -2, so C equals 5. There are 5 chickens on Farmer Brown' s farm. 5.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 50, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0104", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 9, "onset_chunk": 8, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So we have to find the greatest possible value of g c d n plus 7 and 2 n plus one for a positive integer n. Let me call this value d. Then d divides both n plus seven and two n plus one. This means that it also divides their difference, which is two times n plus one minus n plus seven, which simplify s to n minus five. So d divide s n plus seven and n minus five. Therefore, it must also divide their difference, which is twelve. That means the greatest possible value of d is twelve, but we need to check if there' s an n where g c d n plus seven and two n plus one equal s twelve. If I let n be eleven, then n plus seven is eighteen, and two n plus one is twenty three. G c d eighteen and twenty three is one, so that doesn't work. If I try n equals fifteen, n plus seven is twenty two, and two n plus one is thirty one. G c d twenty two and thirty one is one again. How about n equals twenty? N plus seven is twenty seven, and two n plus one is forty one. G", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0114", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 25, "onset_chunk": 24, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so we have the fraction 3 1 divided by 1 110 9. Let' s see what happens when we divide these numbers. I m agine doing long division: 111 09 goes into 3 1 zero times, so we add a decimal point and some zero s. It goes into 3 10 zero times, then 31 00 still zero times, and 3 10 00. Now 111 09 goes into 31 00 twice because two times 1 110 9 is 22 21 8. Sub tract that from 31 00, and we get 8 7 82. Bring down a zero, making it 8 7 8 20. Now, 1 11 09 goes into 87 8 20 seven times because seven times 1 11 09 is 7 7 7 63. Sub", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0126", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 10, "onset_chunk": 9, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So we're looking for the mod ulo 7 remainder of this sum, right? And so let's see if we can simplify things a bit. First off, notice that every third number in this sequence is congruent to 3 mod 7. Right? So let' s group these up into triplets. We have 1 plus 3 plus 5, which is 9. Then 7 plus 9 plus 11, which is 27. And then 13 plus 15 plus 1 7, which is 4 5. Now, when we take each of these mod 7, we get 2, 6, and 3 respectively. And since there are 67 terms in total here, we have 2 2 complete triplets with one extra term left over. The sum of all those triplets mod 7 would be 2 plus 6 plus 3 times 22. Let's compute that: 2 plus 6 is 8, plus 3 times 22 is 66. So 8 plus 66 is 7 4. Now, 7 4 mod 7 is actually 4. But don't forget about that last term! It's 1 99. What is 19 9 mod 7? Well, 1 99 divided by 7 gives us 2 8 with a remainder of 3. So", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0144", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 19, "onset_chunk": 18, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So we have a polyno mial with integer coefficient s and distinct in teger roots, right? And we know that the leading coefficient is 2 and the constant term is 66. So let's think about what this means for the roots. If the polynomial has integer coefficients and distinct integer roots, then by the Integer Ro ot Theorem, each root must divide the constant term. So the possible integer roots are the factors of 6 6. Let' s list them out: plus or minus 1, 2, 3, 6, 1 1, 2 2, 33, and 6 6. Now, since the leading coefficient is 2, the product of the roots taken one at a time should equal 6 6 divided by 2, which is 33. But wait, if all the roots were positive, their product would be positive, but 33 isn't divisible by any combination of these factors without repeat ing a factor or having negative numbers involved. Actually, let me correct that: the product of the roots for a monic poly nomial equals the constant term, but here it's scaled by the leading coefficient. So the product of the roots is 6 6 divided by 2, which is 3 3. Now, 33 can be factored as", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0160", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "n_q_chunks": 60, "onset_chunk": 59, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so this is a phase equilib rium problem between two solid phases of tin. We know the densities and the m olar entropies at equilibrium at 1 bar and 18 degrees Celsius. And we need to find the temperature at which they' re in equilibrium at 35 0 bar. So first off, I'm going to write down the Gibbs free energy equation for each phase. For gray tin, it's G sub g ray equals S sub gra y times T minus P sub gra y times V sub gray plus constant. And similarly for white tin, G sub white equal s S sub white times T minus P sub white times V sub white plus constant. Since we're looking for the temperature where the two phases are in equilibrium, their Gibbs free energies must be equal. So I can set these two equations equal to each other. That gives me S sub gray times T minus P gra y times V gray equal s S sub white times T minus P white times V white. Now I can rearrange that to solve for T. Bring ing all the terms involving T to one side and everything else to the other side, I get T times S gra y minus S white equal s P gray times V gray minus P white times V white. Div", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
